### What are switch expressions?

Switch expressions (JEP 361, final in Java 14) let `switch` return a value. The arrow form `case X -> result;` has no fall-through, so you never need `break`. You can group labels with commas: `case SAV, CUR -> ...`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class InterestCalculator {

    enum AccountType { SAVINGS, CURRENT, FD, RD, NRI }

    public BigDecimal getRate(AccountType type) {
        return switch (type) {
            case SAVINGS -> new BigDecimal("3.5");
            case CURRENT -> BigDecimal.ZERO;
            case FD      -> new BigDecimal("7.1");
            case RD      -> new BigDecimal("6.5");
            case NRI     -> new BigDecimal("4.0");
            // Exhaustive: all enum constants covered. No default needed.
        };
    }
}
```

### Why is fall-through elimination important?

Classic switch with colon syntax falls through to the next case unless you write `break`. Forgetting `break` is one of the most common Java bugs.

```java
// WRONG: classic switch with forgotten break
String description;
switch (type) {
    case SAVINGS:
        description = "Savings account";
        // MISSING break! Falls through to CURRENT!
    case CURRENT:
        description = "Current account";
        break;
    default:
        description = "Unknown";
}
// description is "Current account" even when type is SAVINGS!

// CORRECT: switch expression, no fall-through possible
String description = switch (type) {
    case SAVINGS -> "Savings account";
    case CURRENT -> "Current account";
    default -> "Unknown";
};
```

### How does yield work?

When a case arm needs multiple statements, use a block with `yield` to return the value.

```java
public BigDecimal calculateInterest(AccountType type, BigDecimal balance) {
    BigDecimal rate = switch (type) {
        case FD -> {
            BigDecimal baseRate = new BigDecimal("7.1");
            BigDecimal seniorBonus = isSeniorCitizen() ? new BigDecimal("0.5") : BigDecimal.ZERO;
            yield baseRate.add(seniorBonus);  // yield returns from the block
        }
        case SAVINGS -> new BigDecimal("3.5");
        case CURRENT -> BigDecimal.ZERO;
        default -> new BigDecimal("4.0");
    };
    return balance.multiply(rate).divide(new BigDecimal("100"));
}
```

### What is exhaustiveness checking?

The compiler requires all possible values to be handled. For enums, every constant must appear. For sealed types, every permitted subtype must be covered.

```java
sealed interface Transaction permits Debit, Credit, Reversal {}
record Debit(BigDecimal amount) implements Transaction {}
record Credit(BigDecimal amount) implements Transaction {}
record Reversal(String txnId) implements Transaction {}

public String describe(Transaction txn) {
    return switch (txn) {
        case Debit d   -> "Debit of Rs." + d.amount();
        case Credit c  -> "Credit of Rs." + c.amount();
        case Reversal r -> "Reversal of " + r.txnId();
        // Adding a new subtype without handling it here is a COMPILE ERROR
    };
}
```

### What is pattern matching switch?

Java 21 (JEP 441) adds type patterns and guarded patterns to switch.

```java
public String classify(Object input) {
    return switch (input) {
        case Integer i when i > 100_000 -> "Large amount: " + i;
        case Integer i                  -> "Small amount: " + i;
        case String s when s.startsWith("SAV") -> "Savings account: " + s;
        case String s                   -> "Text: " + s;
        case BigDecimal bd              -> "Decimal: " + bd;
        case null                       -> "Null input";
        default                         -> "Unknown: " + input;
    };
}
```

### How is a switch expression different from a switch statement?

| Aspect | Switch Statement | Switch Expression |
|---|---|---|
| Returns value | No | Yes (assign to variable) |
| Fall-through | Yes (colon syntax) | No (arrow syntax) |
| Break needed | Yes | No |
| Exhaustiveness | Not enforced | Enforced by compiler |
| Pattern matching | Not supported | Supported (Java 21) |
| yield | Not available | Returns value from block |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: mixing arrow and colon syntax
int result = switch (type) {
    case SAVINGS -> 1;
    case CURRENT: yield 2;  // COMPILE ERROR: cannot mix arrow and colon
};

// WRONG: using return instead of yield
int result = switch (type) {
    case SAVINGS -> {
        return 1;  // WRONG: return exits the METHOD, not the switch
    }
    default -> 0;
};
// CORRECT: yield exits the switch block
int result = switch (type) {
    case SAVINGS -> {
        yield 1;   // yields value to the switch expression
    }
    default -> 0;
};
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Switch expressions over sealed types with record patterns give algebraic-data-type style modeling in Java.
- `null` can be handled explicitly: `case null -> ...`. Without it, a null input throws NullPointerException.
- Guard conditions use `when`: `case String s when s.length() > 10 -> ...`. Guards are evaluated after the type match.
- Dominance ordering: specific patterns must come before general ones. `case Integer i when i > 0` must precede `case Integer i`.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you use switch expressions with strings? (Yes, `case "SAV" -> ...` works.)
- What happens if you add a new enum constant? (All switch expressions over that enum fail to compile until the new constant is handled.)
- Is the arrow switch always an expression? (No, you can use arrow syntax in a switch statement too, but it won't return a value.)
